      "writtenOn": "2022-03-29T21:08:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "AsCoeff supports three modes that my approach doesn\u0027t support:\n\n        { SkBlendModeCoeff::kOne,     SkBlendModeCoeff::kOne  }, // plus\n        \nMy code doesn\u0027t clamp the result, and I didn\u0027t want to introduce an extra op that only helped in one case. But it we wanted to wrap the whole thing in `min(vec4(1), ...)` I could support Plus with the current approach. If we think it\u0027s a net win to add in Plus, that SGTM.\n        \n        { SkBlendModeCoeff::kZero,    SkBlendModeCoeff::kSC   }, // modulate\n        { SkBlendModeCoeff::kOne,     SkBlendModeCoeff::kISC  }, // screen\n\nI don\u0027t have any space left to add the signal for SC/ISC. I\u0027m not quite sure how I could fit in the signal for SC/ISC without consuming another uniform float, or making the equation significantly more complex. \n\nActually, that\u0027s not entirely accurate. I can get down the current implementation to a vec3 by adding in two more calls to max, freeing up a slot for the SC/ISC signal. (It was this way at Patchset 6.) Unfortunately, that impacts my ability to support the Plus op (since the vec3 version doesn\u0027t have a way to signal a One coefficient on both the source and dest simultaneously)\n\nI guess, having typed this all out, I think I could probably support either Plus or Modulate/Screen and make it work, at the cost of a few extra ops. To support all three at the same time, I\u0027m not quite sure, unless we\u0027re willing to burn more uniform space. I can keep mulling it over and see if anything comes to me.",
